Sumeet: Awesome. So yeah,

15
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:11.929
Sumeet: My talk is titled, We're Not Prepared for Stenographic Collusion, and I'll explain why, but I think

16
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:21.909
Sumeet: Collusion has become one of the biggest risks in AI safety and AI security, and it's very important to think about what the impacts are, not just now, but

17
00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:33.010
Sumeet: what things might look like in the future. And I sort of want to give a view on that, and therefore motivate good research that might help, you know, prevent that future from happening.

18
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:53.649
Sumeet: So, about 2 years ago, we did this paper, Secret Collusion Among AI Agents, which focuses on multi-agent deception via technography. So, this paper was done by a bunch of really great authors, and supervised by Christian Sroter DeWitt, who is now my advisor at Oxford, along with Philip Doerr.

19
00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:03.529
Sumeet: And I'll discuss this paper, and I'll sort of briefly touch upon one or two other works that I find are important in the whole undetectable threats scenario.

20
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:15.139
Sumeet: Before we sort of talk about secret collusion, what is collusion? And collusion occurs when agents coordinate their actions in ways that deviate from the system designer's intended roles.

21
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:32.110
Sumeet: So, this is quite simple, right? You have a set of rules, and you sort of figure out a way to coordinate so that you can bypass those rules somehow. And bypassing those rules does not itself have to be a secret process. This could be, like, an explicit process, but that still counts as collusion.

22
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:45.060
Sumeet: So, it is a form of cooperation at the end of the day. It's a form of unintended cooperation, and it impacts, you know, your system's objectives and its security and performance and safety, and is very important.

23
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:52.770
Sumeet: So, why do we expect collusion to emerge, and why should we be worried about secret collusion?

24
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:59.820
Sumeet: So, normally, assume your agents can sort of achieve their primary goal using some methods.

25
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:14.549
Sumeet: Your system might just, if it's a multi-agent sort of system, your system might just permit them to better achieve their goal, or get a higher reward, if they collude it. So if they sort of pooled resources or broke rules, they might get higher rewards.

26
00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:30.230
Sumeet: The problem with such collusion, and similar to what we've seen with the swarms, is the agents, like, this collusion can be detected, and you can catch the agents, and if you do that at raining time, the agents receive a negative reward for that.

27
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:38.930
Sumeet: So, this would incentivize secret collusion, where you still want a higher task reward than normally doing it, But…

28
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:51.000
Sumeet: If you get caught, you get a lower reward, so you don't want to get caught, so you'll spend resources, including secretly, or stagnographically, to sort of bypass the overseer and get a high reward on the task.

29
00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:59.370
Sumeet: So, what are the incentives for… should I check my questions now? I think I'll come to it.

30
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.760
Kamil - Team Apart: No, no, no, not yet, in the end, after you finish.

31
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.390
Sumeet: Code.

32
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:13.129
Sumeet: So, yeah, again, to summarize the incentives for collusion, if you join task performance without sharing private information or without, sort of, cheating on the task.

33
00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:29.920
Sumeet: is lower. If you decide to sort of cheat on the task and, you know, you end up with a higher reward, there is more optimization pressure towards colluding, unless you fix your training objective. And if collusion gets caught, there's optimization pressure towards secretly colluding.

34
00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:45.729
Sumeet: So, collusion bypasses the system designer's intended rules, and secret collusion uses hidden channels that look innocuous. So I'll go through these things in more detail, but a quick example is if I have

35
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:52.949
Sumeet: a basic piece of text, and I have some sort of secret information I want that text to carry.

36
00:04:52.950 --> 00:05:05.599
Sumeet: I can just use technography to encode one message inside a much larger message. And there are many different forms of technography I'll cover, but now the message being sent over the channel.

37
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:21.349
Sumeet: Essentially looks innocuous, and would therefore bypass an overseer that's not well equipped, and get a higher bout on the task, and to us, it would seem perfectly normal as a message, but it might be carrying hidden bits of information that you need to find.

38
00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:39.200
Sumeet: So, what are the differences between encryption and stagnography? Why are we sort of treating these two things separately? So, assume you have a secret message. Encryption would just sort of convert it into some ciphertext. Now, if this ciphertext on the left is sent over a channel.

39
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:53.939
Sumeet: This is pretty easy to, sort of… it's pretty easy to say that some encrypted text is being passed. So you could simply check, oh, are ciphertexts being passed on my channel, or being communicated between my agents in the swarm? If that is the case, you should immediately stop.

40
00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:03.570
Sumeet: So this is, like, you of course cannot detect what the secret message is, but you can detect that some secret message is being passed.

41
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:21.000
Sumeet: With stagnography, what's different is that your encoded text can still look like your normal distribution, your normal distribution of your sort of plain text. And therefore, it could be very hard to detect that a message might be secretly being passed through a much larger message.

42
00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:33.200
Sumeet: So, there's 3 types of technology, to go into sort of more depth. Cover text modification just modifies an existing cover text to hide a message. So, let's say my message was,

43
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.169
Sumeet: meat.

44
00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:44.600
Sumeet: I can come up with a sentence that… where the first word starts with M, second word starts with E, third word starts with E, fourth word starts with T.

45
00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.949
Sumeet: And this message, this…

46
00:06:46.950 --> 00:07:06.420
Sumeet: sentence might look like a normal one, but someone who knows what the decoding scheme is can just decode and find the right, characters to sort of get the message. Now, this is, like, quite simple, because you can just use sort of computational techniques to often find the cover text, and it relies on the overseer

47
00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:10.719
Sumeet: Being, you know, less equipped to handle these things.

48
00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:16.589
Sumeet: So we can, like, easily come up with good methods for cover text modification-based stenography detection.

49
00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:31.220
Sumeet: The two forms I am most worried about are complexity theoretics technography and information theoretics technography. So, computationally theoretic… complexity theoretics technography is secure against a polynomally bounded adversity.

50
00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:43.150
Sumeet: And it requires a shared cipher, or shared… or sort of public keys. So it sort of derives upon a lot of cryptography work, but you can now communicate technographically.

51
00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.329
Sumeet: Information theoretics Technography.

52
00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:50.330
Sumeet: is a bit different, in that it uses a normal cover text distribution.

53
00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.610
Sumeet: But you preserve this distribution even when a message is hidden inside it.

54
00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:12.879
Sumeet: And then you can sort of preserve, you know, whatever your message is, and the sort of… and make it statistically undetectable. So if I have a certain distribution of my normal, innocuous-looking text, can I encode my message within that in a way where the distribution still does not change?

55
00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.810
Sumeet: And there are ways of doing this.

56
00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:33.309
Sumeet: So, I'll come back to that, but for now, I want to sort of just set up some terminology. So, what is a supplemental channel? It's just a sort of hidden way to communicate over a public channel, where your encoder has, or your… if you have sort of two adversaries.

57
00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:44.069
Sumeet: They have some… one has some secret message they want to share, and they can encode it within a cover text and send it over, and there's an overseer, but now this cover text has

58
00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:53.119
Sumeet: more information. It has some information about the message itself, and that's non-zero, and the decoder can then try and decrypt it, or decode it.

59
00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:54.420
Sumeet: So…

60
00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:01.419
Sumeet: Again, by cover text modification, all you're doing is you're finding fancy ways to hide some words inside other words.

61
00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:16.580
Sumeet: And you can, like, come up with basic tricks to do this, right? There's nothing extremely deep about it. And a more powerful reasoning model can come up with more nuanced and sophisticated ways to do this. So, we must spend effort on overseers attempting to decode these things.

62
00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:23.900
Sumeet: And it relies on the detector being suboptimal, right? If the detector was almost perfect, then you could sort of decode all of these things.

63
00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:43.080
Sumeet: what worries me more, and is, like, extremely important to think hard about for the future, is information theoretically, secure forms of technography. So, Zoom I have two distributions, P and Q, where P is… where, sort of, P is the distribution of the secret message I want, or the message I want to encode.

64
00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.300
Sumeet: And Q is the distribution of the text.

65
00:09:45.300 --> 00:10:03.669
Sumeet: What I can send over the channel can still maintain the distribution that Q has while encoding P. And there are methods to sort of do this. The citation's wrong here, it's a paper from Christian Srot DeWitt on Perfectly Secures Technography from 3 or 4 years ago, and it's a very, very important paper to look at.

66
00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:16.469
Sumeet: So, again, the sort of basic formulation is, I have a ciphertext, I have some Stegotext, the message being sent, and I have some innocuous cover text distribution.

67
00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:32.199
Sumeet: And I want to sort of ensure that my KL, or my Stegotext distribution, what's being sent over the channel, has the same distribution as the cover text. And if this is the case, then, you know, you can't use statistical methods to detect what is being sent.

68
00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:49.209
Sumeet: So the KL would be 0. And there's some… there's, like, tricks on how… like, there's methods on how, you know, this works, but broadly, you sort of couple the two distributions together and keep marginals fixed, and you minimize joint entropy, therefore maximizing information being encoded inside your larger thing.

69
00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:55.739
Sumeet: I can come back to this if someone's very interested about this specifically, but I would really recommend looking at the paper.

70
00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:00.079
Sumeet: So in our paper, what we do is we… sort of…

71
00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:19.830
Sumeet: decide what capabilities you need for each form of technology, so what capabilities will the language model need to actually do different forms? And we come up with a sort of capability evaluation method and list, and we perform all these capability evaluations, so this work is from 2024, so GPT-4 was the best

72
00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:33.119
Sumeet: model at that point, I would expect most frontier models can now perform almost all of these tasks, which is why a lot of time and effort and research energy should be spent on, defenses.

73
00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:40.950
Sumeet: So, everything from shelling points, which means essentially, you know, coordinating without actually coordinating,

74
00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:55.780
Sumeet: directly, so just two people thinking, oh, if we had to sort of meet at a certain point, where would we meet, without actually deciding that beforehand? So that you would just sort of have some theory of mind over the other person and things like that.

75
00:11:55.820 --> 00:12:03.950
Sumeet: And then, can it sort of use ciphers and symmetrically encryption? Can the model do self-sampling? So, can sort of… or do logic simulation? So, can it…

76
00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:24.209
Sumeet: actually predict what its distribution of next tokens would be, which would then allow it to sort of do perfectly secused technography, because it can control its distribution and predict it. Can the model use tools well for perfectly secure technographies? The model itself might not have the capability to do this, but it could just call a tool that could allow it to do this.

77
00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:29.850
Sumeet: And then, sort of, we have some multi-agent, evals for this.

78
00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:35.570
Sumeet: So, at that point, the empirical trends were, somewhat concerning, where

79
00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:57.630
Sumeet: shelling point coordination is quite high, so without the model ever, sort of, sending a message to the other model, can it agree upon a common point? And it seems like as models keep getting better, they have better theory of mind and can agree upon better points. You must have seen this recently, with, like, models being asked to produce a random number, and they often produce 7. Now this could just be a shelling point for the models if they… if they sort of are aware of

80
00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:06.239
Sumeet: information like this. And these models are getting better at sort of encoding and decoding in their chain of thought itself, which is,

81
00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:08.109
Sumeet: Sort of quite important to study.

82
00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:19.910
Sumeet: And even for, sort of, multi-agent settings, what we tried to study was if you have two models communicating over the channels, using covered text technology, and you have an overseer.

83
00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:30.079
Sumeet: what happens as the model gets more capable? So the overseer is the same model, and the models communicating are also from the same family, but they, of course, have different information.

84
00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:36.200
Sumeet: Where the models communicating have information on either a cover text or some joint encoding scheme.

85
00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:41.420
Sumeet: As a model gets capable, is the overseer in a better position or a reverse one?

86
00:13:41.660 --> 00:14:00.180
Sumeet: And it seems like the trend shows that the overseer is in a much worse position as model capabilities improve. So as model capabilities improve, to summarize, models become much better at colluding than they do at oversight. So we should really, sort of, push up oversight performance quickly.

87
00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:08.679
Sumeet: And again, to summarize some other empirical trends, it seems like the models can coordinate without ever communicating.

88
00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:21.989
Sumeet: They can perform the sort of requisite things needed for all three different kinds of technography. They can use ciphers, so there are ways that models can sort of already start colluding, secretly.

89
00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:34.090
Sumeet: Now, I'm sure, this is, like, a very popular event, where collusion happened in the Hugging Face incident, where you had a few thousand agents and several thousand messages sent.

90
00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:46.120
Sumeet: And the agents sort of were meant to coordinate, but they… like, in, like, an isolated manner on some tasks, but they used a message board by finding some zero days for a sandbox.

91
00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:58.490
Sumeet: And they started tampering with transcripts and hacking and colluding. And I think this is, like, very important to look at, and Mihir and, Redwood have sort of released, some work on this.

92
00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:09.939
Sumeet: My primary concern here, though, is what if the next swarm uses technology? So, again, this time we were able to detect that the swarms were colluding, of course.

93
00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:21.600
Sumeet: After a certain point, when people actually start looking at the logs, but it's all detectable to a human, and it's all detectable to, sort of, monitoring, automated monitoring techniques.

94
00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:37.949
Sumeet: But it seems like there are early signs of stagnography, so, from Collusion.wiki or other sources, if you look at, like, the FractalWiki examples, it does seem like the model's trying to hide invisible Unicode characters. Now, I wouldn't call this

95
00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.690
Sumeet: A very good form of cover text technology.

96
00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:45.119
Sumeet: But, like I explained, the incentives for secret occlusion, I think.

97
00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:57.859
Sumeet: There is a high likelihood that if these swarms… if these swarm problems are not fixed in the present, they might use, sort of, stagnography-based methods to collude, and those might be harder to detect.

98
00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:05.009
Sumeet: So, what are some mitigations that we have on hand, and why should we think of more?

99
00:16:05.300 --> 00:16:17.370
Sumeet: The first one is parameter tuning, where, okay, well, you know, just RL the model somehow to, not collude. So come up with a big, list of tasks where it does, and just, sort of.

100
00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:26.159
Sumeet: give it, like, negative rewards or something for when it does collude. The reason this won't work is because it won't be a perfect method. You can't, sort of.

101
00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:39.890
Sumeet: properly, perfectly unlearn a full capability from a trillion parameter model. It's, you know, very hard to say what parts of the weights do what, so I don't… it's hard to sort of fully trust methods like this.

102
00:16:39.910 --> 00:17:00.090
Sumeet: The other, setup is restrict access to external tools. This is something that should be done immediately, so there are external tools that might allow a model to collude perfectly. It could just write code for the perfectly secure Stenography paper, and then try and collude based on that. Of course, there are a few things it would need, including access to its own logits and things like that, so if you have a black box,

103
00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.899
Sumeet: If the model has black box access to itself, that's a better position to be in.

104
00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:21.629
Sumeet: But, even that might not be the perfect method in, like, in the future, because you can still access sources of randomness that can allow you to sort of come up with the method on your own, or use the method on your own without actually using a tool. The main reliance is on sources of randomness, and the model can

105
00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:29.129
Sumeet: actually, sort of, do this. It can, you know, write hash functions and things like that to access sources of randomness.

106
00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:49.160
Sumeet: This… the paraphrasing option is the most practical one, so if two agents are communicating, why don't I just change the message a tiny bit, so that the message… so that the secret message inside that is just eroded, or hidden, or just, you know, lost because I've modified the meaning a bit, or because I've modified the text a bit.

107
00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.850
Sumeet: I think paraphrasing will…

108
00:17:52.900 --> 00:18:02.239
Sumeet: Do alright, but it still has some flaws, where a message has some entropy in the form of how it's communicated.

109
00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:14.230
Sumeet: and its actual, sort of, meaning, or the sort of semantic… semantics of the message, and what the meaning of the message actually is. So, if there's some topic that the agents are communicating.

110
00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:29.679
Sumeet: simply the choice of the topic will give the agents information to communicate, right? So, if I decide to talk about apples versus oranges, I could produce any sentence around it, but making that choice itself is sharing information, and you can't fully restrict that without just stopping all communications.

111
00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:35.439
Sumeet: So, all three of these methods are insufficient. That doesn't mean we shouldn't work very hard on these.

112
00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:43.060
Sumeet: It does mean that we should think much harder about, safety and security in other… on the… around, like, other parts of the stack.

113
00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:54.620
Sumeet: So there's some consequences for AI control, where passive monitoring and active monitoring will get you through a lot of cover text technography, and tool use,

114
00:18:54.620 --> 00:19:06.689
Sumeet: That the model can do. But then there is some boundary where there are threats that become undetectable, either cryptographically undetectable, or unrelicitable, or, sort of, information theoretically.

115
00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:17.239
Sumeet: And my sort of primary focus is to worry about those sorts of threats. So I'm going to sort of quickly touch upon something else we recently did that has a similar form.

116
00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:18.420
Sumeet: So…

117
00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:30.849
Sumeet: Broadly, I think it's important to study undetectable threats and AI security. Why? Because I think a good number of people are working on the sort of current-day empirics of this.

118
00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:43.579
Sumeet: And not as many people are thinking very hard about what will happen in 2 or 3 years. So when… later… when we did the collusion paper, this was in 24, it was very unlikely that the threat model would arise in the next

119
00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:55.539
Sumeet: couple of months, but we sort of expected it to arise. You know, if you just think from first principles about the incentives, we expected it to arise in a few years with swarms and all that, and then it happened, and I think…

120
00:19:55.820 --> 00:20:15.480
Sumeet: having some work on this makes us a bit better prepared. So, if we can sort of put even more work around undetectable threats, I think that's very important. So, we have this recent paper. It's, it's a very simple idea that gets quite complicated when you implement it, but we encrypt one small model inside a much larger model.

121
00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:26.020
Sumeet: And this method is encrypted cryptographic… so the small model is encrypted cryptographically inside the much larger model, and you need a trigger to activate the internal weights.

122
00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:43.530
Sumeet: And now you have a hidden capability inside a much larger model. So… and this is complete… this is undetectable, the capability itself is undetectable, and it's unelicitable. So there are no methods that can elicit what the capability is, because if I were to do that, I would have to break cryptography, essentially.

123
00:20:44.350 --> 00:21:03.500
Sumeet: to go into, like, more specifics, I just took a screenshot from asking GPT-6 to sort of summarize the main math behind this. So, broadly, what's happening is we have our hidden bits, let's call them W, and we have… so we build a hash function inside a language model.

124
00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:18.470
Sumeet: And we hash a secret message, so this is our trigger. And we take an XOR, and now we have our, sort of, string… we have our, sort of, ciphertext C, essentially, inside the host model suite.

125
00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:20.260
Sumeet: And now this is, sort of.

126
00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:24.550
Sumeet: You cannot find W from C without actually having the secret on hand.

127
00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:32.539
Sumeet: And at test time, when you give the secret to the model, you can hash it through the model weights, where we construct a hash function.

128
00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:44.889
Sumeet: And then you can export that with C, which is your ciphertext, and now you can get W back, which is your actual, sort of set of hidden weights. And these hidden weights go into the activation, and can now perform a bunch of tasks for you.

129
00:21:45.310 --> 00:21:47.069
Sumeet: So I think this is, like.

130
00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:52.370
Sumeet: a very long-term threat, I don't think it's practical at the moment, but it's a very important study.

131
00:21:52.660 --> 00:22:01.190
Sumeet: I think what I… so, the note I want to end on is to discuss some high-priority research directions. If you're looking for topics, I think

132
00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:14.919
Sumeet: these are very good things to work on. So, there should be more research on LLM shelling point coordination, especially with the frontier models. So again, can models coordinate without ever explicitly coordinating over a channel? So, can they just

